00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:06.630
<v Anand>that you may put on WhatsApp, or you may speak over a

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:10.250
<v Anand>call, or a Google Meet discussion, which is not available to every

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:15.370
<v Anand>agent. And when you say, "Look, based on this, I think there

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:20.850
<v Anand>will be a lot more token consumption in the course," then you

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:22.430
<v Anand>have some extra information.

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:26.430
<v Anand>So, that's one of the questions in TDS: can you forecast the

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:30.510
<v Anand>number of tokens that will be consumed in the current week?

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:31.270
<v Kusuma>Okay.

00:26:31.270 --> 00:26:35.130
<v Anand>Based on your knowledge of what people are doing, who's doing what,

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:36.070
<v Anand>how much, et cetera.

00:26:36.070 --> 00:26:39.610
<v Anand>So, that's almost like learning to play in a market where you

00:26:39.610 --> 00:26:42.930
<v Anand>can learn from sources the agents cannot.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:44.410
<v Anand>That's one.

00:26:44.650 --> 00:26:49.670
<v Anand>Another thing we may want to teach is, today agents can't really

00:26:49.670 --> 00:26:54.310
<v Anand>present to a human like a human does, because we want to

00:26:54.310 --> 00:26:55.410
<v Anand>hear a human story.

00:26:57.200 --> 00:27:00.220
<v Anand>Simply because the audience is a human, they want the other side

00:27:00.220 --> 00:27:01.160
<v Anand>to be a human.

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:04.620
<v Anand>So, can you take what an agent has created and narrate it

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:09.900
<v Anand>well? It may simply be take the agent's script, but can you

00:27:09.900 --> 00:27:13.700
<v Anand>recite it in a way that the human listening on the other

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:15.200
<v Anand>side likes?

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:16.700
<v Kusuma>Feels it natural, yes, yes.

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:19.380
<v Anand>Yes. There are technical capability limitations.

00:27:19.460 --> 00:27:22.840
<v Anand>We cannot, for instance, create 3D models well enough yet.

00:27:22.840 --> 00:27:26.040
<v Anand>Next term this won't be a question, because it's at the cusp

00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:28.780
<v Anand>of being solved. But for this term, that is the boundary.

00:27:28.780 --> 00:27:32.360
<v Anand>And therefore, can you figure out, using agents, how to go about

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:35.480
<v Anand>dealing with one of the limitations of agents?

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:42.070
<v Anand>It cannot the technology to, using machine learning, morph from one

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:46.660
<v Anand>face to another is very robust, but not all agents know which

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:51.580
<v Anand>algorithm works well. Can you work with it to do that research,

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.860
<v Anand>to figure out what algorithm works?

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:56.940
<v Anand>There's a big difference in the quality of morphings that I see.

00:27:56.940 --> 00:27:59.320
<v Anand>So, as you said, can I

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:03.160
<v Anand>guide an agent either because it doesn't know or it cannot get

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:09.500
<v Anand>some information, etc. Can I use that agent's output to convince humans,

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:13.340
<v Anand>which is still a human task, are the kinds of tools that

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:15.400
<v Anand>are left for humans in data science.

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:17.650
<v Anand>That's what the course is trying to teach.

00:28:17.650 --> 00:28:21.340
<v Kusuma>Okay, now it kind of makes sense, whatever you told.

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:26.520
<v Kusuma>Also, yeah, in one of your recent blogs, you mentioned that specifying

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:28.900
<v Kusuma>and verifying matters more than executing.

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:33.700
<v Kusuma>So, how does a early learner build that skill to identify when

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:34.840
<v Kusuma>the AI is wrong?

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:41.800
<v Anand>It's hard. When I was in consulting, I would do a

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:46.680
<v Anand>fair bit of analysis at BCG and take that to Alpesh, who

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:47.847
<v Anand>was my project manager.

00:28:47.847 --> 00:28:52.460
<v Anand>He would just quickly look at this, and within a second or

00:28:52.460 --> 00:28:58.500
<v Anand>two, "This number is rubbish." "Why?" "Look, this is

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:00.600
<v Anand>more than the total population of India.

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:03.005
<v Anand>How can there be more consumers than the total population of India?"

00:29:03.005 --> 00:29:04.105
<v Anand>"Right, good

00:29:05.350 --> 00:29:05.850
<v Anand>point."

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:11.030
<v Anand>I actually made a blunder like that when working with the Bank

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:16.830
<v Anand>of India. One of the recommendations that I had proposed was, when

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:20.330
<v Anand>somebody gives you a demand draft, that money stays with you until

00:29:20.330 --> 00:29:22.030
<v Anand>the person the other person collects it.

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:22.620
<v Kusuma>Yes.

00:29:22.510 --> 00:29:23.530
<v Anand>That gives you interest.

00:29:23.530 --> 00:29:25.970
<v Anand>Now, there is no limit to the amount of interest you can

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:29.350
<v Anand>earn. Big draft, lots of interest.

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:32.590
<v Anand>But you're collecting a certain amount of fees.

00:29:32.910 --> 00:29:37.790
<v Anand>Fixed fees, 100 rupees, 10 rupees, depending on the bank and whatever.

00:29:37.990 --> 00:29:42.050
<v Anand>So, my proposal was remove that fee, and you get all of

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:46.310
<v Anand>the interest because everybody will give their demand draft deposits to you.

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:50.250
<v Anand>Later on, I verified that calculation and found that if all of

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:53.830
<v Anand>India's demand drafts were deposited only through Bank of India, they will

00:29:53.830 --> 00:29:55.410
<v Anand>still make less money than the fees.

00:29:56.330 --> 00:29:57.777
<v Anand>Completely stupid, right?

00:29:57.903 --> 00:30:02.330
<v Anand>But this is one example where consulting, as a profession, has discovered

00:30:02.330 --> 00:30:08.550
<v Anand>these shortcuts. Here's how you cross-check somebody else's work without necessarily verifying

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:10.430
<v Anand>the full details of that.

00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:12.390
<v Anand>This is what every auditor does.

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:15.523
<v Anand>They come in from the outside, they cross-check.

00:30:15.737 --> 00:30:19.110
<v Anand>Double-entry bookkeeping: does the left side tally to the right side?

00:30:19.110 --> 00:30:20.590
<v Anand>So, they're doing two things.

00:30:20.810 --> 00:30:23.990
<v Anand>One, they are saying you have to follow a specific process.

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:28.110
<v Anand>The drug regulator is also saying, "Look, if you follow this process,

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:29.230
<v Anand>then I will certify your drug.

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:31.630
<v Anand>If you don't follow the process, I will not certify your drug."

00:30:31.630 --> 00:30:36.610
<v Anand>So, that's one way of even telling any system—human, agent, whatever.

00:30:36.710 --> 00:30:40.410
<v Anand>And then, an outside auditor comes in and inspects and checks if

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:43.570
<v Anand>it is right. How does a judge, who knows nothing about science

00:30:43.570 --> 00:30:46.090
<v Anand>or discovery, rule on a patent case?

00:30:46.090 --> 00:30:48.170
<v Anand>They hear arguments on both sides.

00:30:48.170 --> 00:30:50.870
<v Anand>They look at the evidence and say, "Okay, this sounds more plausible."

00:30:50.870 --> 00:30:56.930
<v Anand>How does, for instance, a wine taster build the skill on

00:30:56.930 --> 00:31:00.170
<v Anand>something that is very subjective?

00:31:00.170 --> 00:31:05.090
<v Anand>How does a police investigator investigate such a wide range of cases

00:31:05.090 --> 00:31:06.210
<v Anand>that come their way?

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:11.090
<v Anand>Where I'm going with this is, over centuries, we have, across different

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:17.530
<v Anand>professions, found various ways of verifying smarter

00:31:17.530 --> 00:31:21.410
<v Anand>intelligences. They could be organizations, they could be societies, they could be

00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:25.630
<v Anand>people with more information than we have, so that information asymmetry comes

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:28.670
<v Anand>in. And the way we do this with agents is not very

00:31:28.670 --> 00:31:31.370
<v Anand>different. We just borrow from all of these professions.

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:37.390
<v Anand>For example, I tell agents, "When you're giving me an output, cite

00:31:37.390 --> 00:31:40.250
<v Anand>the source and quote verbatim from the source."

00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:40.900
<v Kusuma>Mmh.

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:42.070
<v Anand>That does two things.

00:31:42.070 --> 00:31:43.690
<v Anand>One, your results improve.

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:44.190
<v Kusuma>Yes.

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:45.610
<v Anand>Second, I can very easily cross-check.

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:46.180
<v Kusuma>Yes, yes.

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:48.850
<v Anand>Click the link, copy this text, is it actually there?

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:51.930
<v Anand>And if it is there 95% of the time, okay, fine, at

00:31:51.930 --> 00:31:54.410
<v Anand>least 95% of the time you're right.

00:31:55.570 --> 00:31:58.670
<v Anand>Or, I ask another model, "This is what this model said.

00:31:58.670 --> 00:32:00.317
<v Anand>What do you think?" Double-check.

00:32:00.523 --> 00:32:01.537
<v Kusuma>Double-check, yeah.

00:32:01.537 --> 00:32:03.610
<v Anand>Triple-check, quadruple-check. Doesn't cost much.

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:08.350
<v Anand>So, how does one pick up these verification skills?

00:32:08.350 --> 00:32:12.690
<v Anand>I think it is partly about seeing how verification is done in

00:32:12.690 --> 00:32:18.130
<v Anand>different scenarios by learning from various professions and applying it in your

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:19.240
<v Anand>case. That's one way.

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:24.750
<v Kusuma>Mmh. Okay. So, usually when people hear that AI is being used

00:32:24.750 --> 00:32:27.590
<v Kusuma>in a in a course, they think that, you know, it's it

00:32:27.590 --> 00:32:31.130
<v Kusuma>sounds like encouraging cheating and collaborating.

00:32:31.270 --> 00:32:34.510
<v Kusuma>So, if that is not the case, what is the line to

00:32:34.510 --> 00:32:38.110
<v Kusuma>that? And what are some things that students must not do, you

00:32:38.110 --> 00:32:40.350
<v Kusuma>know, within the course?

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:49.390
<v Anand>When people from college join, join Gramener, join Straive, the

00:32:49.390 --> 00:32:54.250
<v Anand>tendency was, I give them a task, they would work very hard

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:57.910
<v Anand>at writing the code to solve the problem.

00:32:57.950 --> 00:32:59.850
<v Anand>Then, I would go back to them and say, "Hold on.

00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:02.630
<v Anand>There is this open-source library that already does this.

00:33:02.630 --> 00:33:03.690
<v Anand>Why didn't you write—"

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:04.270
<v Kusuma>Yes.

00:33:04.270 --> 00:33:09.690
<v Anand>"Why did you write this?" "I wrote it." What they're really saying

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:11.250
<v Anand>is, "Somebody else wrote it.

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:14.530
<v Anand>Me taking their work: A, feels like copying."

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:15.450
<v Kusuma>Mmh.

00:33:15.450 --> 00:33:17.330
<v Anand>"B, then it is their work.

00:33:17.590 --> 00:33:18.760
<v Anand>What am I doing?

00:33:18.760 --> 00:33:21.850
<v Anand>I'm useless. You won't pay me a salary if I don't do

00:33:21.850 --> 00:33:23.090
<v Anand>it by myself."

00:33:23.270 --> 00:33:24.230
<v Kusuma>Yes, yes.

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:26.530
<v Anand>"Boss, I want it done faster.

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:28.270
<v Anand>I want it done cheaper.

00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:32.490
<v Anand>I don't care whether it is 100% your effort or 0% your

00:33:32.490 --> 00:33:35.250
<v Anand>effort. If you get it done, I am perfectly happy.

00:33:35.290 --> 00:33:38.330
<v Anand>What do I have to do to train you to get the

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:43.930
<v Anand>job done? Because my job is to make money at low cost,

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:48.110
<v Anand>high margin. And you trying to reinvent the wheel is not helpful."

00:33:48.130 --> 00:33:49.390
<v Kusuma>High margin. Mmh. True.

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:51.730
<v Anand>"If you have any questions, why don't you reach out to your

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:57.030
<v Anand>colleagues?" "Okay, sir." And a week later, I go back and say,

00:33:57.030 --> 00:34:02.190
<v Anand>"Did you ask your team members for help?" "No." "Why?" I think,

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:07.870
<v Anand>behind the scenes, there is the decades of training saying asking someone

00:34:07.870 --> 00:34:11.190
<v Anand>else a question is cheating.

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:14.170
<v Anand>I'm not saying that that is bad.

00:34:14.170 --> 00:34:16.670
<v Anand>I'm saying that it exists partly for a reason.

00:34:16.670 --> 00:34:20.310
<v Anand>In an academic institution, we are trying to test an individual's ability.

00:34:20.750 --> 00:34:24.270
<v Anand>If two people are collaborating, I find it hard to test that

00:34:24.270 --> 00:34:27.870
<v Anand>individual's ability. So, I, as a teacher, am making my life easy

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:30.410
<v Anand>for me saying, "I will not allow you to collaborate.

00:34:30.430 --> 00:34:32.570
<v Anand>If you do, I will punish you.

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:35.990
<v Anand>And I will test your ability." Now, I have fulfilled my objective,

00:34:35.990 --> 00:34:40.510
<v Anand>which is testing your ability and making sure that you, as an

00:34:40.510 --> 00:34:41.610
<v Anand>individual, have learned.

00:34:41.950 --> 00:34:43.450
<v Anand>What have I not done?

00:34:43.450 --> 00:34:48.310
<v Anand>Made you a productive person in a team setting.

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:51.790
<v Anand>The more productive way is, "Look, 10 of you are there.

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:53.950
<v Anand>I don't want 10 of you solving the same homework.

00:34:53.950 --> 00:34:56.410
<v Anand>I want 10 of you creating 10 new inventions."

00:34:57.990 --> 00:34:59.710
<v Anand>And TDS is aimed at

00:34:59.710 --> 00:35:03.450
<v Anand>doing that. It is telling people, "Look, get the job done.

00:35:03.510 --> 00:35:07.310
<v Anand>If you can ask your friends to do the same thing that

00:35:07.310 --> 00:35:09.910
<v Anand>you were asked to do, it is efficient."

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:10.700
<v Kusuma>Efficient.

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:14.790
<v Anand>Now that begs the question, then, how am I able to assess

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:17.270
<v Anand>individual learning? That is my problem, not your problem.

00:35:18.260 --> 00:35:23.870
<v Kusuma>Yeah, speaking of which, individual learning, you know how an online degree

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:26.450
<v Kusuma>works? I mean, you know, we have assignments and then, you know,

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:30.090
<v Kusuma>if one person completes an assignment, and that is being forwarded in

00:35:30.090 --> 00:35:31.950
<v Kusuma>the groups, and then everyone does that.

00:35:31.970 --> 00:35:35.950
<v Kusuma>So when it comes to like that, how do you understand whether

00:35:35.950 --> 00:35:37.670
<v Kusuma>a student is actually learning?

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:40.570
<v Kusuma>So like, what are the questions you would like to ask them

00:35:40.570 --> 00:35:44.810
<v Kusuma>to understand, "Yes, this student have learned so and so parts of

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:46.870
<v Kusuma>the course." So how do you figure that?

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:50.450
<v Anand>Which begs the question, what do I want to teach?

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:55.370
<v Anand>Supposing a student has done the assignment, let us say that student

00:35:55.370 --> 00:36:01.090
<v Anand>has paid 1,000 rupees to some institute who does the homework and

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:02.970
<v Anand>gives it to them.

00:36:03.230 --> 00:36:04.570
<v Anand>Have they learned something?

00:36:04.810 --> 00:36:07.910
<v Anand>One answer is: no, because I want to teach them how to

00:36:07.910 --> 00:36:11.310
<v Anand>do the homework. Second answer is: absolutely.

00:36:11.450 --> 00:36:16.250
<v Anand>They are trading off 1,000 rupees, which they can afford, for homework

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:19.030
<v Anand>and saved that time, invested it in heaven knows where else, but

00:36:19.030 --> 00:36:20.490
<v Anand>it's probably more productive for them.

00:36:20.650 --> 00:36:23.130
<v Anand>I would absolutely hire the guy, cuz as long as you can

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:25.310
<v Anand>keep spending the 1,000 rupees getting the homework done, now I am

00:36:25.310 --> 00:36:29.350
<v Anand>getting your time and that person's time because you're spending out of

00:36:29.350 --> 00:36:31.370
<v Anand>your own pocket. Yes, I will hire you.

00:36:32.810 --> 00:36:35.810
<v Anand>If someone has figured out the art of being able to collaborate

00:36:35.810 --> 00:36:38.910
<v Anand>and get 10 people to do their work, I will absolutely hire

00:36:38.910 --> 00:36:41.510
<v Anand>you. Because when you come into my organization, I can give you

00:36:41.510 --> 00:36:43.930
<v Anand>10 people's work, and you will farm it out to the right

00:36:43.930 --> 00:36:45.350
<v Anand>people, somehow get it done.

00:36:45.990 --> 00:36:51.110
<v Anand>Fantastic. Not only that, you can, by this collaboration, give me additional

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:53.990
<v Anand>feedback, tell me what other kinds of tasks you will be able

00:36:53.990 --> 00:36:56.470
<v Anand>to do, get the job done.

00:36:56.830 --> 00:37:00.630
<v Anand>So it then begs the question, what am I trying to teach?

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:04.190
<v Anand>I am trying to teach how you get the job done.

00:37:04.500 --> 00:37:05.110
<v Kusuma>Yes.

00:37:05.110 --> 00:37:07.210
<v Anand>And how do how can I evaluate that?

00:37:07.210 --> 00:37:09.310
<v Anand>By making sure that everybody gets the job done.

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:13.830
<v Anand>Now, most teachers have, therefore, the problem: "If I give the same

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:17.370
<v Anand>exercise to everyone, then everybody will give one person will solve it,

00:37:17.370 --> 00:37:20.790
<v Anand>everybody gives back the same exercise." Is that happening in TDS?

00:37:21.110 --> 00:37:25.010
<v Anand>For many years it was, and I would tell people, "You can

00:37:25.010 --> 00:37:27.510
<v Anand>collaborate." And yet there were students who were getting zero.

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:28.610
<v Kusuma>Yes, yes.

00:37:28.670 --> 00:37:31.210
<v Anand>Boss, it is simple.

00:37:31.210 --> 00:37:34.170
<v Anand>There are some questions which don't even change across students.

00:37:34.650 --> 00:37:37.990
<v Anand>If one student solved it, all you have to do is find

00:37:37.990 --> 00:37:40.890
<v Anand>one student who has solved it and get the answer.

00:37:40.890 --> 00:37:44.570
<v Anand>Why are you getting a zero on this?

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:49.550
<v Anand>Now, if that student exists, my guess is as long as there

00:37:49.550 --> 00:37:52.910
<v Anand>are some people who have not yet learned something, but there are

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:55.390
<v Anand>some students who have learned something, there is something to learn.

00:37:56.530 --> 00:37:57.650
<v Anand>Now, what is it?

00:37:57.650 --> 00:38:00.330
<v Anand>It may simply be knowing whom to ask.

00:38:00.370 --> 00:38:02.520
<v Anand>It may also be solving that question, which is also fine.

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:04.880
<v Anand>I'm not fussed about how they learn.

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:07.970
<v Anand>The point is there are various ways of solving it.

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:16.690
<v Anand>So in short, to your question, even with "cheating",

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:20.630
<v Anand>as you put it, how is it that we can measure learning?

